#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221156 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221440 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "41b1a655-6092-57b6-8f95-396c566e7a83" :question/math-academy-id "e-21445" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\},\n\\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ and we have \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-2s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+2)\\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=t^2+3t+2.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+2) & =(t+2)^{2}+3(t+2)+2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4t+4\\,+\\,3t+6\\,+\\,2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+7t+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\{1\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+2)\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}+7t+12\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+7L\\{t\\}+12L\\{1\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+7\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+12\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-2s}\\,L\\{g(t+2)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221166 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221441 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "88ee94b3-1f6e-587a-a63f-736f9df7186e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21582" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\},\n\\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t -4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-4s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+4)\\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)= \\cos(9\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =cos\\,(9\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\pi t+36\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡36\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡9\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡36\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡9\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\right\\}=\\dfrac{s}{s^2 + \\omega^2}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+4)\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+81{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L\\{g(t+4)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+81{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221205 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22ba3ca-cf7a-5a77-abf6-8620076552bb" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Form of the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "1b27851d-3630-506e-972c-b6cef8dbeae3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21864" :question/problem "Find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-1)(t-1)^3 \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\big\\} = e^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-1)(t-1)^3.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a = 1.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-1) = (t-1)^3.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-1)$ with $t{:}$\n\n$$\nf(t) = t^3.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function using the rule $\\mathcal{L}\\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}{:}$\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^3\\} = \\dfrac{3!}{s^{3+1}} = \\dfrac{6}{s^4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\big\\} = e^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor $e^{-s}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-1)(t-1)^{3}\\} & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221440 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222fc96b-f993-5033-9b11-3853bb9b0ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551741} {:answer/id #uuid "249f52eb-e6b8-5ead-84a8-e12e7c704d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551750} {:answer/id #uuid "355d7f8c-1f84-5928-b0b4-128f1331c999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551792} {:answer/id #uuid "49fa1bef-d451-58e5-b74b-92e9470f7657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551843} {:answer/id #uuid "6eed3a49-fbbf-5d50-8f05-9d594624b855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "222fc96b-f993-5033-9b11-3853bb9b0ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551741} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d5c96bc-6dbc-5836-9d52-e4d31072c4c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07fc3c3a-eefd-5543-8284-0927f58d76cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-326384" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L\\{u(t-1)({t}^{2}+1)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-1)({t}^{2}+1)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-1)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-s}\\,L\\{g(t+1)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}+1.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+1) & =(t+1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+2t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+1)\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}+2t+2\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+2L\\{t\\}+2L\\{1\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-1)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-s}\\,L\\{g(t+1)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221440 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c517300-501c-5f66-8dd1-40ee6f04f3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302738} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff16169-dbd7-53d6-a0f6-015ab8384793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302757} {:answer/id #uuid "46c314d9-669a-5794-b001-364e7fb33a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302905} {:answer/id #uuid "a50dc0f5-e95d-558b-a189-82772df5145d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303471} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a6ef5c-1c49-5828-946b-daf17f03eafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{-1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ff16169-dbd7-53d6-a0f6-015ab8384793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302757} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d5ff6dd-95cc-5416-ae98-7823d1849772" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f92e95f-43ab-59a9-949c-e31e679c7c82" :question/math-academy-id "q-331017" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L\\{u(t-6)({t}^{2}-t+3)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-6)({t}^{2}-t+3)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=6,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-6)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-6s}\\,L\\{g(t+6)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}-t+3.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+6) & =(t+6)^{2}-(t+6)+3 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+12t+36\\,-\\,t-6\\,+\\,3 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+11t+33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+6)\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}+11t+33\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+11L\\{t\\}+33L\\{1\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+11\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+33\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-6)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-6s}\\,L\\{g(t+6)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221440 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bb27a9c-9274-505c-b809-dc931adf0cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{51}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551724} {:answer/id #uuid "836b6858-1620-5a9e-adb3-522412d11a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-7s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551977} {:answer/id #uuid "927e93bf-b6b8-5e43-b513-b25ebf9ba37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-7s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{51}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552021} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd681bd-c83f-5b14-a500-0f755f3bfbcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-7s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{0}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552045} {:answer/id #uuid "aca76f57-5d16-538e-9db1-62eaf57bf9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "927e93bf-b6b8-5e43-b513-b25ebf9ba37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-7s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{51}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552021} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbcb8564-443f-5a87-ab9d-12627df755da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fe4c9be-d1a0-58fd-a27b-7c4761a3ccb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-331018" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L\\{u(t-7)({t}^{2}+2)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-7)({t}^{2}+2)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=7,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-7)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-7s}\\,L\\{g(t+7)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}+2.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+7) & =(t+7)^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+14t+49\\,+\\,2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+14t+51.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+7)\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}+14t+51\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+14L\\{t\\}+51L\\{1\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+14\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+51\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{51}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-7)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-7s}\\,L\\{g(t+7)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-7s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{51}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221440 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b22c3c8-246d-53fa-a3d3-a39b18ba2cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302729} {:answer/id #uuid "4919f7b1-698d-527f-9dac-092a0992e68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302915} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4599f2-8b8d-5d66-8234-d3c49a7ef211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{-2}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303248} {:answer/id #uuid "977ad4c6-cdb9-5ca0-b4b7-e17e3149ff0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303395} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3cf0aa-e5c0-5aa5-93c4-602a5f1c27e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b22c3c8-246d-53fa-a3d3-a39b18ba2cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302729} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc980550-ee73-5cb8-8125-19a252a97e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2f62fcb-3c2f-57e7-b9f2-f62f3e2f0d4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-331025" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L\\{u(t-11)({t}^{2}-2t+5)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-11)({t}^{2}-2t+5)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=11,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-11)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-11s}\\,L\\{g(t+11)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}-2t+5.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+11) & =(t+11)^{2}-2(t+11)+5 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+22t+121\\,-\\,2t-22\\,+\\,5 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+20t+104.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+11)\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}+20t+104\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+20L\\{t\\}+104L\\{1\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+20\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+104\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-11)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-11s}\\,L\\{g(t+11)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221440 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a2fb05b-1d25-5425-ba47-85863cf2f923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{42}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551721} {:answer/id #uuid "1b79da6e-ef80-5c9b-97f7-7f34b2f3f742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551723} {:answer/id #uuid "2483bd78-fe24-50d5-8f64-2b05e6a9f556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551749} {:answer/id #uuid "3e24d06a-d81f-51fe-acca-e6fb6d2df509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{42}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551817} {:answer/id #uuid "f26644ca-17c4-5890-976c-53e0f3cf0198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e24d06a-d81f-51fe-acca-e6fb6d2df509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{42}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551817} :answer-field/id #uuid "89062eb0-dbed-56e9-9ef7-1cd526b82f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c235044-2675-5c70-a69d-3ac1bca6c5ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-331027" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L\\{u(t-4)({t}^{2}+6t+2)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-4)({t}^{2}+6t+2)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-4s}\\,L\\{g(t+4)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}+6t+2.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =(t+4)^{2}+6(t+4)+2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+8t+16\\,+\\,6t+24\\,+\\,2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+14t+42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+4)\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}+14t+42\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+14L\\{t\\}+42L\\{1\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+14\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+42\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{42}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L\\{g(t+4)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{42}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221441 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73615501-75a8-5a0d-ae42-5a5baa81c031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303153} {:answer/id #uuid "9933a85c-b338-5c59-bc0f-c6ccaa03146f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303406} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d4e5af-f5b3-5d8c-9eff-4d717e22b60d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303497} {:answer/id #uuid "cf25f9c3-58af-52c3-ac83-c9fdad7c1e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+4{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303734} {:answer/id #uuid "e3165b4c-3ad9-5a08-9d70-2d92c07b7f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9933a85c-b338-5c59-bc0f-c6ccaa03146f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303406} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d09784f-1642-59bd-831a-f9fb74d842e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "477fdf0a-8165-5144-ad0b-72ada4a489e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331040" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L\\{u(t-2)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-2)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-2s}\\,L\\{g(t+2)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+2) & =sin\\,(\\pi (t+2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t+2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+2)\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-2s}\\,L\\{g(t+2)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221441 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182cce37-e813-530f-98fb-0166e11c6e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302615} {:answer/id #uuid "5bec99c0-cf71-588b-8408-c82ee7075312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303017} {:answer/id #uuid "694800b8-f95c-5340-9666-c59d00da26e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+9{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303097} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5783a5-8163-5806-a9ad-7baa90d54d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/id