5\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡20\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡20\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+4)\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L\\{g(t+4)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221441 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b44799b1-1927-5693-8329-a62ff21c4c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372623} {:answer/id #uuid "3856e06f-360b-5f6d-b125-c9544e064d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+7{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372624} {:answer/id #uuid "36c15ab5-ca38-50de-a938-ba12d2199834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372625} {:answer/id #uuid "254f6703-144f-556e-a6cb-2fbb6f34e3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372626} {:answer/id #uuid "333371c4-3ad7-50e4-931a-58b8c46aa5a5" :answer/type {:db/id #e